  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Inocybe tubarioides, commonly known as Tubaria-like Fiberhead, is a small woodland mushroom belonging to the Fibercaps family, characterized by its fibrous, radially-streaked cap and distinctive stem features. Like many members of its genus, this species is toxic and should never be consumed.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap texture and color",
      "description": "Brown to ochre-brown cap with prominent radial fibrils giving it a distinctive streaked appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills and spore print",
      "description": "Gills are attached and tan to rusty-brown, with spore print ranging from cinnamon to rust-colored",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem characteristics",
      "description": "Thin, fragile stem that is whitish to pale brown with a fibrous appearance, often with a subtle bulbous base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Inocybe tubarioides grows in mixed woodlands throughout New England, typically appearing on rich, moist soil and leaf litter from summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Members of the Inocybe genus contain muscarine, a toxic alkaloid that affects the nervous system, and this species has been a subject of mycological study to better understand toxin distribution across Fibercap species.",
